1
00:00:00,378 --> 00:00:03,907
In your notes, you drew this graph, and two ways to lay it out.

2
00:00:03,907 --> 00:00:06,889
Let us give it a story: a morning routine.

3
00:00:06,889 --> 00:00:08,204
A is shower.

4
00:00:08,204 --> 00:00:09,649
B is get dressed.

5
00:00:09,649 --> 00:00:11,094
C is coffee.

6
00:00:11,094 --> 00:00:12,383
D is leave.

7
00:00:12,383 --> 00:00:16,442
An arrow means: this task must come before that one.

8
00:00:17,345 --> 00:00:20,066
Now lay the four tasks on a line.

9
00:00:20,066 --> 00:00:26,056
In this order, C, B, A, D, three arrows point backward.

10
00:00:26,056 --> 00:00:28,244
You would drink coffee before showering.

11
00:00:28,244 --> 00:00:34,220
In this order, A, B, C, D, every arrow points the same way.

12
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:36,746
That is called a topological order.

13
00:00:36,746 --> 00:00:40,427
Your note says: a DAG always has one.

14
00:00:41,342 --> 00:00:42,814
Why a DAG?

15
00:00:42,814 --> 00:00:47,931
Suppose get dressed had to come before
shower, and shower before get dressed.

16
00:00:47,931 --> 00:00:50,457
No order could satisfy both.

17
00:00:50,457 --> 00:00:53,721
With no cycles, an order always exists.

18
00:00:54,637 --> 00:00:55,991
A bigger one.

19
00:00:55,991 --> 00:01:00,080
Priya is cooking dinner: seven tasks, and twelve rules.

20
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:05,275
An arrow from one task to another means
the first must be finished before the second

21
00:01:05,275 --> 00:01:06,213
starts.

22
00:01:06,213 --> 00:01:09,204
In what order can Priya do everything?

23
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:19,109
Here is a trick, using depth-first search.

24
00:01:19,109 --> 00:01:21,127
When you reach a task, start it.

25
00:01:21,127 --> 00:01:25,086
Then visit everything it unlocks, before anything else.

26
00:01:25,086 --> 00:01:27,442
Only then, call this task finished.

27
00:01:27,442 --> 00:01:31,557
So a task always finishes after everything that depends on it.

28
00:01:31,557 --> 00:01:32,569
Watch.

29
00:01:33,489 --> 00:01:34,752
Start at Shop.

30
00:01:34,752 --> 00:01:37,148
Shop unlocks Chop, so go there.

31
00:01:37,148 --> 00:01:39,075
Chop unlocks Boil.

32
00:01:39,075 --> 00:01:40,846
Boil unlocks Sauce.

33
00:01:40,846 --> 00:01:42,708
Sauce unlocks Plate.

34
00:01:42,708 --> 00:01:44,349
Plate unlocks Eat.

35
00:01:44,349 --> 00:01:48,941
Eat unlocks nothing, so Eat is the first task to finish.

36
00:01:50,489 --> 00:01:51,869
Now back up.

37
00:01:51,869 --> 00:01:55,242
Plate has nothing left, so Plate is finished.

38
00:01:55,242 --> 00:01:56,817
Sauce is finished.

39
00:01:56,817 --> 00:02:00,606
Back at Boil, its other task, Plate, is already finished,

40
00:02:00,606 --> 00:02:01,789
so skip it.

41
00:02:01,789 --> 00:02:03,556
Boil is finished.

42
00:02:04,467 --> 00:02:05,730
Back at Chop.

43
00:02:05,730 --> 00:02:08,816
Its next task, Fry, is new, so go there.

44
00:02:08,816 --> 00:02:12,423
Fry's tasks, Sauce and Plate, are both finished:

45
00:02:12,423 --> 00:02:13,504
skip them.

46
00:02:13,504 --> 00:02:14,845
Fry is finished.

47
00:02:14,845 --> 00:02:17,827
Chop's last task, Sauce, is done too.

48
00:02:17,827 --> 00:02:19,324
Chop is finished.

49
00:02:19,324 --> 00:02:22,306
Back at Shop, Boil and Fry are done.

50
00:02:22,306 --> 00:02:24,464
Shop finishes last of all.

51
00:02:25,405 --> 00:02:29,533
The finish order is Eat, Plate, Sauce, Boil, Fry,

52
00:02:29,533 --> 00:02:30,913
Chop, Shop.

53
00:02:30,913 --> 00:02:32,696
That is called the postorder.

54
00:02:32,696 --> 00:02:38,412
Reverse it: Shop, Chop, Fry, Boil, Sauce, Plate,

55
00:02:38,412 --> 00:02:39,168
Eat.

56
00:02:39,168 --> 00:02:43,813
Lay the tasks out in that order, and all twelve arrows point forward.

57
00:02:44,696 --> 00:02:46,037
Why does it work?

58
00:02:46,037 --> 00:02:48,485
Look at the arrow from Fry to Sauce.

59
00:02:48,485 --> 00:02:50,699
Sauce finished before Fry did.

60
00:02:50,699 --> 00:02:55,386
And at the arrow from Chop to Boil: Boil finished before Chop.

61
00:02:55,386 --> 00:02:59,253
For every arrow, the task it points to finishes first.

62
00:02:59,253 --> 00:03:04,627
So when the finish order is reversed, every
task comes before the tasks it points to.

63
00:03:05,514 --> 00:03:07,676
What about the order the tasks start?

64
00:03:07,676 --> 00:03:11,660
That is called the preorder: Shop, Chop, Boil, Sauce,

65
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:13,821
Plate, Eat, Fry.

66
00:03:13,821 --> 00:03:19,225
Look: Fry comes last, but Fry must come before Sauce and Plate.

67
00:03:19,225 --> 00:03:21,400
Two arrows point backward.

68
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:24,313
The preorder is not a topological order.

69
00:03:25,256 --> 00:03:27,925
Back to the dinner, with one more detail.

70
00:03:27,925 --> 00:03:30,386
Priya also wants to set the table.

71
00:03:30,386 --> 00:03:35,556
Setting the table has nothing before it, and its only arrow leads to Eat.

72
00:03:35,556 --> 00:03:38,655
Now there are two tasks with nothing before them:

73
00:03:38,655 --> 00:03:40,191
Shop, and Table.

74
00:03:40,191 --> 00:03:43,889
So a loop over all the tasks could start at either one.

75
00:03:43,889 --> 00:03:45,370
Let us run both.

76
00:03:46,288 --> 00:03:49,765
Case one: the dictionary lists Shop first.

77
00:03:49,765 --> 00:03:53,866
The search runs exactly as before, and Shop finishes.

78
00:03:53,866 --> 00:03:56,783
Then the loop reaches Table, a new task.

79
00:03:56,783 --> 00:04:00,559
Its only arrow leads to Eat, which is already finished.

80
00:04:00,559 --> 00:04:03,146
So Table finishes last of all.

81
00:04:04,071 --> 00:04:07,795
Case two: the dictionary lists Table first.

82
00:04:07,795 --> 00:04:09,240
Table starts.

83
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:12,873
Its arrow leads to Eat, a new task, so Eat starts,

84
00:04:12,873 --> 00:04:16,232
and finishes at once, since it unlocks nothing.

85
00:04:16,232 --> 00:04:18,238
Then Table finishes.

86
00:04:18,238 --> 00:04:22,001
Now the loop reaches Shop, and the whole dinner search runs.

87
00:04:22,001 --> 00:04:26,411
But Eat is already finished, so every arrow into Eat is skipped.

88
00:04:27,363 --> 00:04:29,746
Here are both cases side by side.

89
00:04:29,746 --> 00:04:31,426
Case two, below.

90
00:04:31,426 --> 00:04:36,738
In both, the start order fails: three arrows point backward each time.

91
00:04:36,738 --> 00:04:41,269
But the reversed finish order is a valid topological order in both.

92
00:04:41,269 --> 00:04:43,001
They are not the same order.

93
00:04:43,001 --> 00:04:45,762
In case one, Table comes first.

94
00:04:45,762 --> 00:04:49,512
In case two, Table comes late, right before Eat.

95
00:04:49,512 --> 00:04:53,414
Both are correct, because Table only has to come before Eat.

96
00:04:54,347 --> 00:04:55,571
Now your code.

97
00:04:55,571 --> 00:04:59,490
It keeps three things: the start order, the finish order,

98
00:04:59,490 --> 00:05:01,574
and a set of visited tasks.

99
00:05:01,574 --> 00:05:07,160
A function inside a function, build topo from, handles one task.

100
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:11,375
And an outer loop tries every task, so every one is covered.

101
00:05:12,285 --> 00:05:17,871
build_topo_from is defined inside topo
order, like nested was in count paths.

102
00:05:17,871 --> 00:05:21,087
So it can read and change pre order, and visited,

103
00:05:21,087 --> 00:05:23,158
and post order, from outside.

104
00:05:23,158 --> 00:05:25,589
Every call shares the same three.

105
00:05:26,488 --> 00:05:28,936
One call, step by step.

106
00:05:28,936 --> 00:05:31,202
Write the task into the start order.

107
00:05:31,202 --> 00:05:32,582
Mark it visited.

108
00:05:32,582 --> 00:05:36,801
For each task it unlocks, if that task is not visited yet,

109
00:05:36,801 --> 00:05:39,014
go and handle it first.

110
00:05:39,014 --> 00:05:43,412
Only after all of them are done, write this task into the finish order.

111
00:05:44,306 --> 00:05:46,064
Where is the base case?

112
00:05:46,064 --> 00:05:47,626
There is no if for it.

113
00:05:47,626 --> 00:05:51,389
When a task has no children, or all of them have been started,

114
00:05:51,389 --> 00:05:53,134
the for loop does nothing.

115
00:05:53,134 --> 00:05:56,780
The function falls through to post order, and returns.

116
00:05:56,780 --> 00:06:01,259
That is what your note says: none of the
recursive calls happen when all the children

117
00:06:01,259 --> 00:06:02,857
have already been started.

118
00:06:03,758 --> 00:06:05,190
The outer loop.

119
00:06:05,190 --> 00:06:09,513
For each node in the dictionary, in the order the dictionary lists them,

120
00:06:09,513 --> 00:06:13,940
if it was not already covered, start a fresh depth-first search from it.

121
00:06:13,940 --> 00:06:18,511
That is the loop from the earlier videos,
and it is why the dictionary order mattered

122
00:06:18,511 --> 00:06:20,044
in the two cases.

123
00:06:20,965 --> 00:06:22,176
Now the bug.

124
00:06:22,176 --> 00:06:24,715
The last line returns pre order.

125
00:06:24,715 --> 00:06:28,504
We just saw that the start order is not a topological order.

126
00:06:28,504 --> 00:06:33,869
The fix: return the reverse of post order, with list of reversed post order.

127
00:06:33,869 --> 00:06:38,856
Reversed hands the items back to front, and list turns that into a list.

128
00:06:38,856 --> 00:06:40,626
This is shown here only.

129
00:06:40,626 --> 00:06:42,628
Your file stays as you wrote it.

130
00:06:43,559 --> 00:06:44,926
Now your test.

131
00:06:44,926 --> 00:06:49,184
Your test_topo_order is a copy of the first test.

132
00:06:49,184 --> 00:06:54,432
It calls topo order with three arguments, but topo order takes only one:

133
00:06:54,432 --> 00:06:55,486
the graph.

134
00:06:55,486 --> 00:06:57,504
The spec's test is different.

135
00:06:57,504 --> 00:06:59,363
It checks the order itself.

136
00:07:00,260 --> 00:07:02,786
The real test, line by line.

137
00:07:02,786 --> 00:07:06,797
Sorted order equals sorted graph: the same vertices,

138
00:07:06,797 --> 00:07:08,086
none lost.

139
00:07:08,086 --> 00:07:13,164
Order dot index of u is less than order dot index of w.

140
00:07:13,164 --> 00:07:15,455
Index gives a position in the list.

141
00:07:15,455 --> 00:07:21,744
For every vertex u, and every child w of u,
all means every one of those checks must

142
00:07:21,744 --> 00:07:22,773
be true.

143
00:07:22,773 --> 00:07:26,129
In other words: every arrow points forward.

144
00:07:27,003 --> 00:07:29,009
Run your function as written.

145
00:07:29,009 --> 00:07:33,567
On the first graph it returns A, B, D, E, C.

146
00:07:33,567 --> 00:07:36,666
C points to D, but C comes after D.

147
00:07:36,666 --> 00:07:38,137
The test fails.

148
00:07:38,137 --> 00:07:44,257
With the fix, both graphs pass: A, C, B, D, E, and then Z,

149
00:07:44,257 --> 00:07:45,581
Y, X.

150
00:07:46,491 --> 00:07:48,041
How fast is it?

151
00:07:48,041 --> 00:07:50,371
Each task is visited once.

152
00:07:50,371 --> 00:07:52,741
Each arrow is looked at once.

153
00:07:52,741 --> 00:07:55,931
So the function runs in V plus E time.

154
00:07:55,931 --> 00:08:00,410
The test uses index, which scans the list, so the test is slower.

155
00:08:00,410 --> 00:08:02,008
The function is not.

156
00:08:02,919 --> 00:08:04,117
To recap.

157
00:08:04,117 --> 00:08:08,258
A topological order puts every arrow pointing the same way.

158
00:08:08,258 --> 00:08:12,151
Depth-first search gives one: reverse the finish order.

159
00:08:12,151 --> 00:08:14,469
The start order is not enough.

160
00:08:14,469 --> 00:08:18,870
Your code needs one change: return the reversed post order.

161
00:08:18,870 --> 00:08:22,867
Next: the book's chapter six, preorder, postorder,

162
00:08:22,867 --> 00:08:24,777
and cycles, in detail.
